  {
    "slide": 14,
    "text": "Alright..now that we have some background understanding of concepts relevant to the cell cycle and division, let’s get into it!",
    "raw_slide_sha256": "bce05db518c490489d214ccfbf2b1382a3d6a85151543e9661c824cbd974e08a",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout2.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide14.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image13.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": "‹#›"
  },
  {
    "slide": 15,
    "text": "The Cell Cycle \nOverview\nInterphase\n → makes up most of the cell cycle, the cell is not dividing\nG1 Phase\n (cell growth and normal metabolic pr\nocesses)\nS Phase\n (\n‘S’ for synthesis → \nDNA \nreplication takes\n place)\nG2 Phase \n(preparation for division)\nAlmost every cell goes through distinct phases and subphases\n2. \t\nM phase (cell division)\nMitosis\n (nuclear division) \nProphase\nMetaphase \nAnaphase\nTelophase\nCytokinesis\n(division of cytoplasm and organelles)",
    "raw_slide_sha256": "481b6fdf4a04248075f10b82a84d13732116973909b353bedcf08af82ff897f8",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout2.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide15.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image12.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 16,
    "text": "Duplicated Chromosomes\nDuring the S-phase of interphase, DNA is replicated to double the genetic content of the cell. This is to ensure each \ndaughter\n cell received a copy of each chromosome and the genes on it.  \nWe can observe this best when looking at \nunduplicated and duplicated chromosomes\n.\nThe two arms of a duplicated chromosome are called \nsister chromatids\n and are exact copies of one another (result of S-phase)\nSister chromatids are held together by the \ncentromere\nBefore S-phase*\nAfter\nS-phase*\n*Note: \nRemember\n that chromosomes are not actually condensed like this until cell division is about the occur, this is just for illustration purposes. ",
    "raw_slide_sha256": "a9bb9d1bcb1bf30b797cd3fab416882208e4054d0425abb77d893de267c20852",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout2.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide16.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image10.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": "‹#›"
  },
  {
    "slide": 17,
    "text": "The Cell Cycle\nM-\nphase\n accounts for the actual division of genetic information (\nmitosis\n) and splitting of the cytoplasm itself (\ncytokinesis\n)",
    "raw_slide_sha256": "c56e4a74215854c2c7e3ecb78e0c37bc8f8e04e20645798f87382d04067ca22f",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout2.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide17.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image20.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 18,
    "text": "If you were to look at a sample of healthy tissues under a microscope, most of the cells you would observe would likely be in interphase/early prophase\n. \nWhy\n do you think that is?\nIf cells spend most of their time (approx 90%) in interphase, the chance of finding a particular cell in interphase is much greater. Therefore most cells should be found in interphase at any time of observation. \nActively growing tissues, such as the the tip of plant roots, however will have a slightly higher proportion of cells in mitosis",
    "raw_slide_sha256": "275470d06704ab654769711e792230949b15fe899a14f48a1b217a40a7d5a567",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide18.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image18.png"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId4",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image26.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 19,
    "text": "What would happen if cells were constantly dividing in an unc\nontrolled fashion\n?\nCANCER",
    "raw_slide_sha256": "779d5e749a0c5af0b0812d343b6d6701504c27023cee69f0c34ca98594825566",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide19.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image19.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "slide": 20,
    "text": "Cancer & the Cell Cycle\nUncontrolled cell division may be caused by exposure to \nmutagens\n, or agents which change an organism’s genetic material. \nMutagens may be physical, chemical, or biological in origin.\nMutagens that specifically lead to the formation of cancerous cells are further classified as \ncarcinogens\n.",
    "raw_slide_sha256": "77e75ccdc41e0193a26e70fd4691b8b028653b4b8dc9996ff8a9bdc8db3d88e3",
    "relations": [
      {
        "Id": "rId1",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/slideLayout",
        "Target": "../slideLayouts/slideLayout3.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId2",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/notesSlide",
        "Target": "../notesSlides/notesSlide20.xml"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId3",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image21.png"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId4",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image27.png"
      },
      {
        "Id": "rId5",
        "Type": "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image",
        "Target": "../media/image33.png"
      }
    ],
    "notes": "‹#›"
  },
  {
    "slide": 21,
    "text": "Mitosis\nThe extent of repair varies from organism to organism\nIs a form of cell division that produces daughter cells that are identical to parent cells.  ",
    "raw_slide_sha256": "1b5cebdd91436d9274b72e31a27f7a06e7608a8965c57acbe348058e4fbca3bf",
    "relations": [
      {
